  "tint": "tint-dogs",
  "title": "How to brush your dog's teeth (without the wrestling match)",
  "shortTitle": "Dog teeth brushing",
  "excerpt": "A five-step routine, the right paste and the treats that make dental care something to look forward to.",
  "date": "19 Aug 2026",
  "datetime": "2026-08-19",
  "readTime": "5 min read",
  "author": {
   "name": "Neha Joshi",
   "role": "Head groomer & trainer",
   "bio": "Certified professional groomer and fear-free trainer with over 10 years experience helping pets feel comfortable with handling and hygiene routines.",
   "initials": "NJ",
   "tint": "tint-dogs"
  },
  "metaDesc": "A five-step routine, pet-safe enzymatic toothpastes and positive reinforcement techniques to make dental care stress-free for your dog.",
  "heroImg": "blog-teeth",
  "heroAlt": "A person gently brushing a relaxed dog's teeth with a small brush",
  "lead": "Over 80% of dogs show signs of dental disease by age three. Yet for most pet families, tooth brushing feels like an Olympic wrestling match. Here is our step-by-step method to turn dental care into an easy, reward-filled daily ritual.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "id": "why-brush",
    "title": "Why brushing matters more than bones",
    "html": "<p>Plaque hardens into tartar in just 48 hours. While recreational chews provide some mechanical friction on the back molars, they cannot reach the delicate gumline where periodontal bacteria cause bone loss and systemic inflammation.</p><ul><li>Prevents painful tooth root abscesses and tooth loss</li><li>Stops bacteria from entering the bloodstream and straining kidneys</li><li>Eliminates chronic bad breath caused by anaerobic bacteria</li><li>Saves thousands in costly veterinary dental procedures under general anaesthesia</li></ul>"
   },
   {
    "id": "tools",
    "title": "The right gear: brushes, pastes & finger caps",
    "html": "<p>Never use human toothpaste. Fluoride and foaming agents like SLS can cause severe stomach upset, and artificial sweeteners like xylitol are fatally toxic to dogs. Always choose a pet-specific enzymatic toothpaste.</p><blockquote>“Enzymatic pastes continue breaking down plaque even after you finish brushing, so you don’t need perfection—just consistent contact with the teeth.” — Dr. Meera Kulkarni, consulting vet</blockquote>"
   },
   {
    "id": "five-day",
    "title": "The 5-day introduction routine",
    "html": "<p>Rushing straight in with a toothbrush is the quickest way to create a mouth-shy dog. Spread the introduction over five low-pressure days:</p><ol><li><strong>Day 1 (The Taste Test):</strong> Let your dog lick a pea-sized dab of poultry or peanut paste from your finger. Praise enthusiastically.</li><li><strong>Day 2 (Lip Lift):</strong> Lift the upper lip gently for three seconds, rub a smear of paste along the gums with your bare finger, and reward.</li><li><strong>Day 3 (Introduce the Brush):</strong> Let them lick paste directly off the bristles so the texture feels familiar and positive.</li><li><strong>Day 4 (Front Teeth):</strong> Gently brush the large canine teeth in small circular motions for 10 seconds.</li><li><strong>Day 5 (Full Mouth):</strong> Work backward along the cheek teeth on both sides. Don't worry about the inside surfaces yet.</li></ol>"
   },
   {
    "id": "technique",
    "title": "Targeting the cheek teeth where plaque hides",
    "html": "<p>The upper fourth premolars—the large teeth right under the eyes—accumulate tartar faster than any other tooth. Focus 80% of your brushing time on the outer surface of these back upper molars.</p><div class=\"tip-box\"><svg class=\"icon\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><path d=\"M12 16v-4\"/><path d=\"M12 8h.01\"/></svg><p><strong>Trainer tip:</strong> You do not need to pry your dog's jaw open. Keep the mouth closed, slide the brush under the cheek, and brush with lips relaxed.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "id": "checklist",
    "title": "Your weekly dental care checklist",
    "html": "<ol><li>Brushing scheduled at the same time daily (after the evening walk works best)</li><li>Weekly visual check for red, inflamed gums or chipped teeth</li><li>VOHC-approved dental chew offered two to three times a week</li><li>Fresh water bowl refreshed daily to reduce oral bacteria</li><li>Annual oral checkup scheduled during your routine vet consultation</li></ol>"
   }
  ],
