  "tint": "tint-cats",
  "title": "What happens at your cat's annual health check?",
  "shortTitle": "Cat's annual checkup",
  "excerpt": "From weight and teeth to vaccines, here's what your vet looks for and how to make the carrier less scary.",
  "date": "11 Aug 2026",
  "datetime": "2026-08-11",
  "readTime": "6 min read",
  "author": {
   "name": "Dr. Meera Kulkarni",
   "role": "Consulting vet · Reviewed by Priya Sen",
   "bio": "BVSc & AH, consulting vet for Wag & Whisker. Meera runs our monthly free puppy clinics and shares her home with two Indies and a very bossy cat.",
   "initials": "MK",
   "tint": "tint-cats"
  },
  "metaDesc": "From weight and teeth to vaccines, here's what your vet looks for and how to make the carrier less scary.",
  "heroImg": "blog-cat-vet",
  "heroAlt": "A white cat calmly being examined by a vet wearing gloves",
  "lead": "Cats are evolutionary masters at concealing illness. In the wild, showing pain or weakness makes an animal a target, so domestic felines quietly suffer through dental pain, arthritis, and kidney changes long before outward symptoms show. An annual exam catches these subtle shifts before they become emergencies.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "id": "carrier",
    "title": "Step one: making the carrier less terrifying",
    "html": "<p>The stress of a vet visit starts an hour before you leave the house. If your carrier only comes out of the closet when it is time for shots, your cat's adrenaline spikes before you even touch the door handle.</p><ul><li>Keep the carrier out in the living room year-round with a soft blanket inside</li><li>Spritz synthetic feline facial pheromones (Feliway) into the carrier 15 minutes before departure</li><li>Place a towel over the carrier in the car to block disorienting motion and headlight glare</li><li>Never carry the carrier swinging from the handle—cradle it with two hands against your chest for stability</li></ul>"
   },
   {
    "id": "exam",
    "title": "The nose-to-tail physical exam",
    "html": "<p>A thorough feline checkup evaluates twelve key physical systems in less than twenty minutes:</p><ul><li><strong>Eyes &amp; Ears:</strong> Checking for retinal clarity, early cataracts, and hidden ear mites or wax buildup.</li><li><strong>Mouth &amp; Gums:</strong> Inspecting for feline odontoclastic resorptive lesions (FORLs), gingivitis, and loose teeth.</li><li><strong>Heart &amp; Lungs:</strong> Listening for faint heart murmurs, arrhythmias, and wheezes.</li><li><strong>Abdominal Palpation:</strong> Feeling the kidneys for size changes, the bladder for thickening, and the bowel loops for discomfort.</li><li><strong>Joints &amp; Spine:</strong> Checking hip extension and lumbar tenderness, especially in cats over seven.</li></ul>"
   },
   {
    "id": "weight",
    "title": "The scale tells a story",
    "html": "<p>For an average 4 kg cat, losing or gaining just 400 grams is the human equivalent of losing or gaining 8 kilograms. Gradual weight loss is often the very first clinical sign of chronic kidney disease, hyperthyroidism, or diabetes.</p><div class=\"tip-box\"><svg class=\"icon\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><path d=\"M12 16v-4\"/><path d=\"M12 8h.01\"/></svg><p><strong>Vet tip:</strong> Weigh your cat monthly at home using a digital baby scale, or step on your bathroom scale holding your cat, then subtract your own weight.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "id": "vaccines",
    "title": "Vaccines, deworming and bloodwork",
    "html": "<p>Indoor cats still need core vaccinations (panleukopenia, herpesvirus, calicivirus and rabies) because viruses can travel indoors on shoes and clothing. For cats seven years and older, annual baseline blood panels (kidney, liver, and thyroid screening) are essential to catch early metabolic changes.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "checklist",
    "title": "Questions to ask at your cat's checkup",
    "html": "<ol><li>Is my cat's current body condition score ideal, overweight, or thin?</li><li>Are there any signs of tartar buildup or gum inflammation?</li><li>Does my cat's hydration level look healthy for their kidney function?</li><li>Are there any subtle mobility changes in the spine or hips I should monitor?</li><li>What is the recommended vaccination schedule based on my cat's lifestyle?</li></ol>"
   }
  ],
