  "heroImg": "blog-guinea",
  "heroAlt": "A brown and white guinea pig nibbling in the grass",
  "lead": "Just like humans, guinea pigs are among the very few mammals incapable of producing their own Vitamin C. Without regular dietary intake of fresh vegetables, guinea pigs rapidly develop scurvy, dental issues, dull coats and painful joint swelling. Here is your definitive veterinary nutrition guide to daily salads, safe treats, and foods to avoid.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "id": "pyramid",
    "title": "The guinea pig diet pyramid",
    "html": "<p>Before planning your salad menu, remember that fresh vegetables are only one piece of the nutritional puzzle:</p><ul><li><strong>80% Unlimited Hay:</strong> High-fibre Timothy, orchard, or meadow hay must be available 24/7 to keep the digestive tract moving and wear down continuously growing teeth.</li><li><strong>15% Fresh Vegetables:</strong> One cup of washed, mixed fresh greens and vegetables per guinea pig daily.</li><li><strong>5% Fortified Pellets:</strong> Plain, high-fibre timothy-based pellets containing stabilized Vitamin C (no seeds, nuts, or colourful cereal pieces).</li></ul>"
   },
   {
    "id": "daily",
    "title": "The daily Vitamin C superstars",
    "html": "<p>These low-calcium, high-vitamin vegetables can be fed every single day without risk of bladder sludge:</p><ul><li><strong>Bell Peppers (Capsicum):</strong> The gold standard. Green bell peppers are lowest in sugar; yellow and red are packed with Vitamin C. Feed a quarter pepper daily.</li><li><strong>Romaine &amp; Green Leaf Lettuce:</strong> Excellent hydration and fibre. Avoid light iceberg lettuce completely.</li><li><strong>Coriander (Cilantro):</strong> Gentle on tummies, widely loved by cavies, and safe for daily munching.</li><li><strong>Cucumber:</strong> High water content, perfect on warm afternoons for hydration.</li></ul>"
   },
   {
    "id": "treats",
    "title": "Weekly treats: watching calcium and sugar",
    "html": "<p>Vegetables high in oxalic acid or calcium should only be fed once or twice a week to avoid urinary calcium stones:</p><blockquote>“Guinea pigs cannot metabolise excess calcium easily. Overfeeding kale, spinach, and parsley often leads to painful bladder stones that require emergency surgery.” — Priya Sen, companion animal nutritionist</blockquote>"
   },
   {
    "id": "danger",
    "title": "What never to feed your guinea pig",
    "html": "<div class=\"tip-box\"><svg class=\"icon\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><path d=\"M12 16v-4\"/><path d=\"M12 8h.01\"/></svg><p><strong>Toxic warning:</strong> Never feed onions, garlic, chives, potatoes, avocado, tomato leaves/stems, chocolate, seeds, or human bread. These cause acute gastrointestinal stasis and toxicity.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "id": "prep",
    "title": "Salad prep & food hygiene rules",
    "html": "<ol><li>Wash all produce thoroughly under running water to remove agricultural pesticides</li><li>Serve at room temperature—cold vegetables straight from the fridge cause painful gas and cramps</li><li>Discard uneaten fresh greens after four hours to prevent bacterial contamination and fruit flies</li><li>Introduce any new vegetable gradually over five days to protect sensitive caecal flora</li></ol>"
   }
  ],
